      },
      {
        "id": "lem-boundary-homology-cokernels-surject-under-excisive-bordism-gluing",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Boundary homology cokernels under bordism gluing",
        "deps": [
          "thm-excision-and-mayer-vietoris-with-local-coefficients"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Cha and Kim, Iterated satellite operators on the knot concordance group",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.04629v2",
              "locator": "Lemma4.13, printed pages17–18"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative unital ring. Let $W$ and $X$ be compact manifolds with supplied boundary collars and decompositions $\\partial W=A\\sqcup D$ and $\\partial X=A\\sqcup B$, where each summand is a union of boundary components. Glue the two copies of $A$ by a supplied homeomorphism, obtaining $Y=W\\cup_A X$ with $\\partial Y=D\\sqcup B$. All homology below has constant coefficients $R$. Suppose\n$$H_1(A;R)\\longrightarrow H_1(X;R)\\ \\text{is injective},\\qquad H_2(B;R)\\longrightarrow H_2(X;R)\\ \\text{is surjective}.$$\nThen inclusion of $W$ induces a well-defined surjection\n$$\\frac{H_2(W;R)}{\\operatorname{im}H_2(\\partial W;R)} \\twoheadrightarrow \\frac{H_2(Y;R)}{\\operatorname{im}H_2(\\partial Y;R)}.$$\nIn particular, if $R$ is a field and the first quotient is finite dimensional, the dimension of the second quotient is at most that of the first. No injectivity assumption on $H_2(A;R)\\to H_2(X;R)$, orientability, or choice principle is needed.",
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-subgroup-group-ring-extension-is-faithfully-flat-and-preserves-fraction-quotients",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Subgroup group-ring extension and fraction quotients",
        "deps": [
          "def-tensor-product-of-modules-by-generators-and-relations",
          "def-homology-object-of-a-chain-complex"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Cha and Kim, Iterated satellite operators on the knot concordance group",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.04629v2",
              "locator": "Equation4.6 and preceding paragraph, printed page15"
            },
            {
              "title": "Cochran, Orr and Teichner, Knot concordance, Whitney towers and L2-signatures",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/9908117",
              "locator": "Section2.1, Ore localization and induced coefficient modules"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Let $k$ be a commutative unital ring and $H\\le G$ a subgroup. Set $A=k[H]\\subseteq B=k[G]$. Extension $-\\otimes_A B$ on right modules is exact and faithful, and for each right $A$-module $M$ the map $m\\mapsto m\\otimes1$ is split injective as an $A$-module map. For every right-$A$ chain complex $C_*$, without a boundedness or finiteness assumption, there is the natural isomorphism\n$$H_i(C_*)\\otimes_A B\\cong H_i(C_*\\otimes_A B).$$\nThe corresponding left-module assertions hold with the tensor factors reversed.\n\nSuppose additionally that $A$ and $B$ have $1\\ne0$, no zero divisors and both Ore conditions, and are embedded in their right-fraction division rings $F$ and $K$, respectively. Then the inclusion $A\\to B$ extends uniquely to an injective ring map $F\\to K$. Under this identification,\n$$F\\cap B=A,\\qquad F/A\\longrightarrow K/B\\text{ is injective as an }A\\text{-bimodule map}.$$\nAll assertions require no choice principle, including when $G/H$ is infinite. No splitting of a group extension is assumed.",
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-compact-manifolds-are-retracts-of-finite-polyhedra-without-choice",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Compact manifolds are retracts of finite polyhedra without choice",
        "deps": [
